 Top of the Morning 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Shoshana Gordon/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 BC Partners yesterday picked up its clubs and walked away from a $300 million investment in LIV Golf, which has been on life support since Saudi Arabia's Public Investment Fund pulled its financial support.

 Don't be surprised if PIF today tells a bankruptcy court that liquidation is the only viable path forward. Behind the scenes: A source familiar with the situation tells me that BC had lined up the required co-investment — around half the total — and that more than 30 players had signed onto the restructuring support agreement to participate in what was dubbed LIV 2.0.

 The problem, I'm told, is that there were too many unsigned players that BC wanted as part of the deal. A sticking point was that players were being offered equity in the new league, whereas some of these unsigned players (or their agents) wanted cash. State of play: The deadline arrived last week and was briefly extended, but PIF didn't want it to extend indefinitely. 

 It's unclear whether BC's co-investors would have moved forward with the existing roster, but now it's a moot point. Neither BC Partners nor LIV Golf is commenting publicly.

 
 
