 Update-a-palooza 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Eniola Odetunde/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Quick updates to some past stories... 

 🇨🇳 IP-uh-oh: Chinese financial regulators were behind OPay's decision to abandon its $304 million IPO after it priced, as first reported by Bloomberg and confirmed by Axios.

 OPay is an Africa-focused payments company founded by Norway's Opera that's headquartered in Singapore. That's likely why neither the company's lawyers nor lead underwriter Citibank sought approval from the China Securities Regulatory Commission. The problem is that OPay has an R&amp;D team based in China, and someone complained just before the IPO priced last Thursday night. CSRC is said to have called Citi on Friday morning, before shares began trading, thus prompting OPay to put the entire offering on ice. Word is that the CSRC is expected to give OPay its blessing to list in the U.S., but it's unclear if that's days, weeks, or months away. Thus the decision to pause indefinitely. ❓ Answer key: We recently asked you to name the family office manager who just spun out and raised an independent fund. Your hint was that the family in question has more than one family office.

 The answer was Elon Boms, who led Pritzker Vlock Capital Management. He's now formed V29 Capital Management as a private equity firm focused on founder-led, tech-enabled businesses. V29 has raised $166 million for its debut fund from LPs like BlackRock, Sagard, Pomona Capital — and bought three assets from PVCM. It plans to raise a second fund shortly. 🍟 Wonder-ful: In May we reported that Wonder, the labor-light restaurant concept led by former Walmart ecommerce boss Marc Lore, was planning to go public in 2027.

 Bloomberg today reports that it's filed confidential IPO papers. In related news, Amazon dumped a delivery agreement with Wonder-owned GrubHub in favor of a deal with Uber. ✨ Data disruption: Yesterday we discussed how the midterms could curb AI growth by restricting the building of new data centers, with a particular focus on governors.

 It's also worth noting that 44 ballot measures in 10 states sought to restrict data centers, and all but one of them passed . Moreover, an average of 75% of voters favored the limits. 
 
